//! Differential-vector test for
//! `crates/irregular-nesting-native/src/archive/reconstruction.rs`.
//!
//! Loads `tests/vectors/reconstruction.json`, generated by
//! `scripts/rust-parity/dump-reconstruction.ts` from the REAL production
//! `intrinsicReconstructionPortfolio.ts`, and asserts this crate's Rust port
//! reproduces every recorded outcome exactly:
//! - Section A: `buildCanonicalEndpointOrders`'s four traversal orders over
//!   small axis-aligned-rectangle piece/placement configurations.
//! - Section B: `buildIntrinsicReconstructionSpecs`'s canonical role order
//!   plus `intrinsicReconstructionSpecMatchesFamily`'s per-family counts.
//! - Section C: `intrinsicReconstructionEffectiveOrderKey`/
//!   `intrinsicPreparedPieceClassKey` over hand-built shapes and real
//!   mixed61 hull rings.
//! - Section D: `retainIntrinsicReconstructionArchive`'s capacity/dedup/
//!   protected-seed behavior over hand-picked run batteries (reproduced
//!   verbatim in this file from `dump-reconstruction.ts`'s own identical
//!   batteries -- these are synthetic, hand-authored inputs to a pure
//!   function, not TS-computed outputs, so no input serialization round-trip
//!   is needed; only the recorded TS *output* `retainedHashes` is compared).
//! - Sections E-H: full `runIntrinsicReconstructionPortfolio` end-to-end
//!   cases. Each case's seed (role, canonical hash, placements, step trace,
//!   metrics) is recorded directly by `dump-reconstruction.ts` and
//!   reconstructed byte-for-byte here (`seed_from_json`) -- **not**
//!   re-derived via a fresh `construct_intrinsic_strict_state` call. This is
//!   load-bearing, not merely a convenience: Section F's
//!   `DUPLICATE_ORDER_BATTERY` seeds are hand-placed via TS's own
//!   `realPlace` at deliberately chosen (non-decoded) coordinates designed
//!   to force `orderOwners` collisions, not the output of a real decode --
//!   recomputing the seed on the Rust side (as an earlier revision of this
//!   file did) would silently substitute a different seed than the one TS
//!   actually ran the portfolio against, for every one of those cases, and
//!   the mismatch would surface only in the specific placement fields the
//!   two derivations happen to agree/disagree on (an earlier revision hit
//!   exactly this: rust=-0/ts=0 on `runs[0].placements[0].translateX`, a
//!   symptom of comparing two structurally different seeds rather than a
//!   real signed-zero bug in the port). Sections E/G/H seeds legitimately do
//!   come from a real decode in TS (`realSeedFrom`) -- but that fact is now
//!   irrelevant to this file, since it always replays the exact recorded
//!   seed either way.

